      "When a conversation happens between two people, it can end. Someone says something, someone else responds, a point lands or it doesn't, and either way, the conversation has a shape — a beginning, a middle, an end. When the conversation is just you with yourself, it has no shape at all. It's a tape loop. You can pause it. You can rewind to the part where you were winning. You can start over from a different opening line tomorrow. The conversation cannot finish, because there is no one in the room who will remember it tomorrow.",
      "The longest argument of your life is the one you've never said out loud.",
      "This is the actual reason most people stay in the relationship with the substance for years longer than they need to. It isn't the chemical hook, although that's real. It isn't the social pressure, although that's real too. It's that the deliberation has been quarantined inside one skull, where it can be infinitely deferred. You have not failed to decide. You have failed to commit the deliberation to a form that can decide. Those are different problems.",
      "The substance, meanwhile, becomes a way to suspend the conversation. Not to answer it. Just to mute it for a few hours. By Tuesday morning, the loop is running again, and you start the day half-believing that this time you'll finish the thought, and you don't, and you reach for the suspension again that evening. Years go by like this.",
      "The thing that changes the conversation isn't a new insight. You already have all the insights. The thing that changes the conversation is putting a second person in the room.",
