      "Sobriety is lonelier than use. This is not a problem to be fixed.",
      "The recovery industry will tell you the opposite. It will tell you to find your sober community, attend the meetings, get the sponsor, fill the calendar. The advice is well-meaning. It is also premature. In the first months of stopping, the loneliness is not a hole to be plugged with new social activity. It is a piece of information that the substance has been keeping you from receiving for years, and the only way to receive it is to sit in it long enough that you can read what it actually says.",
      "What it actually says, for most people, is some version of: a number of your friendships were transactions. They were transactions in which the substance was the medium of exchange. You met at the bar, or you smoked together, or you used together, and the using was the substrate of the relationship — not the topic, not the reason you became friends, but the table the friendship was set on. When you remove the table, the friendship is still there, but it has nowhere to sit. Some of these friendships will adapt. Some will not. The ones that don't aren't fake. They were real friendships. They just had a substrate, and the substrate is gone.",
      "You will not understand this in week one. In week one, you will think your friends are abandoning you. In week six, you'll start to see that you've stopped inviting some of them, and they've stopped inviting you, and it's because the thing you used to do together is the thing you no longer do, and neither of you has yet figured out what else there is.",
