      "None of these things are peaks. They are plains. The plains feel, at first, like a vast and unentertaining flatness. You will sit in your apartment at four in the afternoon on a Tuesday and think: this is not fun. You will be correct, by the substance-fun metric. You will also be wrong, because the metric is wrong.",
      "The metric is wrong because it measures only intensity. It does not measure duration, accessibility, or recovery time. A 9-out-of-10 peak that lasts three hours and takes a day to recover from is, by total volume, less pleasure than a sustained 6-out-of-10 that lasts a week. You weren't trained to see this. The substance trained you to chase 9s and discount 6s. After several years of that training, the 6s have stopped registering as pleasure at all, and you've concluded that life without 9s is life without pleasure.",
